<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paxton Boys</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1763">1763</a>–<a href="/years/1764">1764</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Paxton Boys were frontier settlers from around Paxton in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> who, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontiac">Pontiac</a>'s War, attacked the peaceful Conestoga Indians, survivors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a>, in December 1763, killing all of them, including those sheltered in the Lancaster workhouse. In 1764 they marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a> helped persuade them to turn back. None were punished.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paxton-boys.jpg" alt="Paxton Boys" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paxton_massacre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Paxton Boys were Scots-Irish Presbyterians from the hill country north-west of Lancaster and across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehanna-river">Susquehanna River</a> in Cumberland County, many of them squatters on Indigenous land whose farms the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> raided during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontiac">Pontiac</a>'s War. In the spring of 1763 Reverend John Elder, the parson of Paxtang known as the Fighting Parson, recruited 110 associators to defend the frontier, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> government would not let them take the offensive.</p><p>Their leaders called the Indians Canaanites who must be destroyed, rejected any distinction between friendly and hostile Indians, and despised the pacifist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> and Moravians whom they blamed for weakening the backcountry. The Conestoga, who had lived peacefully at Conestoga Town for decades, had shrunk to seven men, five women and eight children by 1763.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Unproven claims that Tenseedaagua, or Will Sock, was aiding hostile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> led Matthew Smith to report armed strangers at the village. At daybreak on 14 December 1763 about 50 Paxton Boys killed and scalped the six Conestoga they found and burned the buildings. On 27 December, led by Smith and Lazarus Stewart, they broke into the Lancaster workhouse and killed the fourteen survivors sheltered there, women and children among them.</p><p>In February 1764 a few hundred marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> to kill the Moravian Lenape and Mohican refugees housed in the city barracks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a> raised volunteer companies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> among them, and on 7 February, after a day of talks at Germantown, the marchers agreed to go home and put their grievances in writing. Their Declaration and Remonstrance demanded a scalp bounty, which the government created later that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Franklin's Narrative of the Late Massacres condemned the killers, but Governor John Penn's reward found no one, and sympathy in Lancaster County prevented arrests. Franklin wrote that the spirit of killing all Indians spread through the country. In January 1768 Frederick Stump and John Ironcutter murdered ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> and Mohicans and were freed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> jail by a mob, and the Black Boys of James Smith attacked pack trains bound for Fort Pitt in 1765.</p><p>From 1770 Stewart led Paxton Boys into the Wyoming Valley for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>'s Susquehanna Company in the Pennamite-Yankee War. They sided with the Patriots in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, and Stewart was killed at the Battle of Wyoming on 3 July 1778.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paxton Boys” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Survivors joined the Iroquois; the last Conestoga were killed by the <b>Paxton Boys</b> in 1763.</blockquote><a href="/state/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a></li></ul></section>
